+# Changes from baseline Metacat 1.2
+
+Every difference between this tree and James Marshall's Metacat 1.2, which is
+the initial commit `5bb07c9`.
+
+The rules this list follows:
+
+- **GUI mode is untouched.** Loading `metacat.ss` gives the original program.
+  Everything below is either behind `%gui-mode%`, in a new file, or a bug fix.
+- **No change alters what the architecture does.** There is no new codelet, no
+  altered formula, no Slipnet change, no adjusted constant. The engine is
+  Marshall's; the fixes let it run headless and stop it aborting runs.
+
+Three kinds, in order of how much they matter to anyone comparing against this
+program's behaviour.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Bug fixes — these change measured behaviour
+
+Each was a message sent to an object that could not answer it. That reaches
+`report-error-and-halt` (`utilities.ss`), which prints `Ooops: bad message ...`
+and `(reset)`s — silently abandoning the run. Under SWL the line went to the GUI
+console and the run just vanished, which is why none of these were noticed
+before the program was sampled at scale.
+
+### 1.1 `constituent-objects-of` excluded workspace-strings — `bf06847`
+
+`utilities.ss`, and its two call sites `rules.ss:1432` and `rules.ss:1540`.
+
+The original code sent `'get-constituent-objects` to every reference object,
+which halts when one is a *letter* — as happens when a rule names a group the
+target string does not have. `130314d` routed both sites through a new
+`constituent-objects-of` that tested `group?`. Excluding letters was right;
+testing `group?` was wider than the bug and also excluded **workspace-strings**,
+which do answer that message, with their top-level objects.
+
+That broke every rule clause naming the whole string — a swap marked as a
+subobjects swap, and Marshall's own `abc → aaa` example at
+`workspace-strings.ss:455-460`. Such clauses denoted nothing, produced no
+transforms, failed `currently-works?` and were never built. Now tests `letter?`.
+
+**Effect on behaviour: large, on five problems.** Re-running the whole oracle,
+`eqe-baaab` moved 0.335 TVD (`baaab` 24.9% → 58.2%), `fig5.4-top` 0.236
+(`ixxi` 4.4% → 27.5%), `run6` 0.124, `misc3` 0.053, `copy5` 0.020. The other
+fourteen moved ≤0.006, i.e. sampling noise.
+
+### 1.2 Nine methods a workspace-string needs as a snag object — `5cb8afc`, `ca8f7e0`
+
+`workspace-strings.ss`, added to the block Marshall had already started with the
+comment *"The following methods allow workspace-strings to behave as spanning
+groups"*.
+
+A snag object can be the whole string, and code then sends it messages only
+letters and groups answer:
+
+| method | reached from |
+|---|---|
+| `get-string` | snag explanation phrasing (`trace.ss:1104,1111,1114`) |
+| `clamp-salience`, `unclamp-salience` | snag display (`trace.ss:1160,1167`) |
+| `get-all-descriptors` | snag concept pattern (`trace.ss:1048`) |
+| `which-string` | snag-event `equal?` → `equivalent-workspace-objects?` (`trace.ss:1152,920`) |
+| `get-group-category`, `get-direction`, `get-group-length` | same, group branch (`trace.ss:923-930`) |
+| `get-descriptions` | jootser gathering snag-object descriptions (`jootsing.ss:86-88`) |
+
+The first four were found during the first oracle run, where they killed four
+`copy*` problems early. The last five need *two* snags in one run, so 374,500
+single runs never hit them; episodes reach them easily, because once Episodic
+Memory suppresses a problem's answers the later runs grind to the codelet
+ceiling and pile up snags.
+
+Found by enumeration rather than one at a time: `report-error-and-halt` was
+temporarily replaced with a recorder returning `#f`, so a run limps on and
+reveals the next missing message. It took three sweeps — returning `#f` corrupts
+the computation, so a run usually dies of a downstream type error before
+reaching the next genuine bad send.
+
+**Effect on behaviour: none where they do not fire.** A problem that records no
+bad message is unaffected; these only convert an abandoned run into a completed
+one.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Headless mode — new files, GUI untouched
+
+Metacat needs SWL, Tcl/Tk and an X display even with the GUI unused. Performance
+mode removes that: no GUI files, no `(setup)`, no display.
+
+| file | |
+|---|---|
+| `metacat-headless.ss` | **new.** The performance-mode loader. Loads the engine and nothing else — none of the sixteen GUI files. |
+| `headless.ss` | **new.** Supplies every GUI dependency as a no-op: thirteen window globals, the drawing procedures the engine calls unguarded, fonts, layout tables, pause variables, SWL primitives. |
+| `metacat.ss` | `%gui-mode% #t`. This is the GUI loader; the flag is what the two modes branch on. |
+| `constants.ss` | Two load-time calls guarded on `%gui-mode%`: `swl-color` needs `<rgb>`, `%logo-font%` needs `swl-font` from `fonts.ss`. Both no-ops in GUI mode. |
+| `run.ss` | One `'garbage-collect` send guarded on `%workspace-graphics%`, like the ~60 other workspace-drawing sites. Unguarded it dies at codelet 100 when graphics are off. |
+
+Three procedures that live in graphics files but are **not** graphics are copied
+verbatim into `headless.ss` rather than stubbed, because engine code calls them
+and uses their results: `find-next-space-position`,
+`group-event-pexp-text-string`, `relation-name`.
+
+`headless.ss` also carries a purpose-built **null memory window** (`ca8f7e0`).
+The generic null window is not enough: an answer-description holds
+`get-normal-icon-pexp` as a *procedure*, initialised to `#f` and installed only
+by the real memory window, and three methods apply it. Leave it `#f` and the
+second run of any session whose first run answered dies with *"attempt to apply
+non-procedure #f"*. Only a session-based harness reaches this, since every
+single-run harness clears memory between runs.
+
+**Measured: 26 → ~2,500 codelets/sec, and no decay.** 100 consecutive runs hold
+5,861 → 5,702 c/s flat; with the GUI loaded the same sequence fell 1,792 → 1,454
+and then off a cliff, because Metacat's windows retag canvas items as garbage and
+rely on a `'garbage-collect` that `run-mcat` only ever sends to two of them.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Performance — no behavioural change
+
+### `tell` as a macro — `17b4683`
+
+`utilities.ss`. `tell` was a procedure taking a rest-argument list; it is now
+`extend-syntax` expanding to a direct call, with the old procedure kept as
+`tell-proc` for the handful of sites that apply it dynamically (`tell-all` and
+friends). Same dispatch, same semantics, no consing per send.
+
+**~2,400 → ~2,900 codelets/sec.**
+
+---
+
+## What is *not* changed
+
+No codelet added, removed or reweighted. No formula, temperature rule, or
+Slipnet node, link or depth altered. No constant tuned. `%distance-threshold%`,
+`%verbatim-rule-probability%`, the abstraction probabilities and every other
+parameter are Marshall's.
+
+The one place a fix changed which rules the program can build is §1.1, and that
+restored a path Marshall's own comments describe rather than adding a new one.
