+# Using the oracle data
+
+See also [`README.md`](README.md) for the repository layout and
+[`CODE-FIXES.md`](CODE-FIXES.md) for how this tree differs from Metacat 1.2.
+
+Metacat carries two measured reference data sets. Both exist to answer one
+question about another program — currently the Petacat port — and it is not
+"is it correct":
+
+> **Has its behaviour changed, and where?**
+
+Metacat is a stochastic, self-watching architecture with no ground truth to
+check against. There is no right answer to `abc → cba ; mrrjjj`. What there is,
+is a *distribution* the reference implementation produces, measured densely
+enough to be stable. A comparison against it says stable or changed. It never
+says pass or fail.
+
+That distinction governs everything below. **Every check flags; no check
+fails.** A flag is an invitation to look, and during parameter and Slipnet
+experiments a flag is frequently the result being sought rather than a defect.
+
+## The two data sets
+
+| file | what it samples | scale |
+|---|---|---|
+| `oracle/raw/single-runs.json` | one run, fresh Episodic Memory each time | 374,500 runs |
+| `oracle/raw/episodes.json.gz` | one *episode* — eight runs in a session, memory carried forward | 9,500 episodes (76,000 runs) |
+
+Those two are the **raw** data — what the reference emitted, never edited. From
+them `docker/make_derived_sets.py` recomputes the four files a comparison run
+actually loads:
+
+| file | |
+|---|---|
+| `oracle/derived/single-run-sets.json` | every stopping state, with shares and `f1_over_n` |
+| `oracle/derived/single-run-p50.json` | the p50 subset, with absence probabilities at n=100 |
+| `oracle/derived/convergence-sets.json` | every episodic convergence answer |
+| `oracle/derived/convergence-p50.json` | its p50 subset |
+
+Both are produced by the reference running headless (`metacat-headless.ss`,
+plain Chez, no SWL, no display) with a 100,000-codelet cap per run.
+
+## How a comparison run works
+
+The program under test runs **100 tries per problem** in each mode. That number
+is deliberate: it is cheap enough to run often, and the checks below are chosen
+so that 100 is enough for them to mean something.
+
+### Single runs — 100 runs per problem, against `single-run-sets.json` / `single-run-p50.json`
+
+1. **Did every p50 state appear?** The p50 set is the fewest most-frequent
+   states whose combined share reaches 50%. Each entry carries
+   `absence_probability_at_n100`, and across all 19 problems that probability is
+   **0.0000** — p50 states hold at least 13% share, so missing one in 100 runs is
+   roughly a one-in-a-million event. A missing p50 state is therefore a real
+   signal, not sampling luck. **Flag it.**
+
+2. **Did any state appear that the oracle never produced?** Sampling continued
+   until the Good-Turing missing-mass estimate `f1/n` fell to 1e-4 or below,
+   meaning the next reference run had about a 1-in-10,000 chance of producing
+   something new, so a state outside the set is very unlikely to be
+   undersampling. **Flag it.**
+
+   Three problems stopped short of that and say so in their `stop_reason`:
+   `fig5.4-top` (0.00037) and `eqe-baaab` (0.00025) hit the 50,000-run
+   `checkpoint` with their state sets still growing, and `misc3` (0.00021) was
+   stopped by hand at `shards_exited`. Even there the cost is under 0.04
+   spurious flags per 100 runs, but a novelty flag on those three carries
+   marginally less weight than on the other sixteen.
+   `docker/check_saturation.py` reports the current figure for every problem.
+
+### Episodes — 100 episodes of 8 runs per problem, against `convergence-sets.json` / `convergence-p50.json`
+
+An episode's **convergence answer** is the last run in it that actually produced
+an answer. Runs that gave up (`*NONE*`) or hit the codelet ceiling (`*CAP*`) are
+skipped when looking backwards for it — they are not answers. An episode that
+never answered contributes nothing; that happens once in 9,500 episodes.
+
+Collect the convergence answers of the 100 episodes into a set, and run the same
+two checks:
+
+1. **Did every p50 convergence answer appear?** These sets are small — one to
+   four answers — and, as with single runs, every member's absence probability at
+   100 episodes is **0.0000**. **Flag a missing one.**
+
+2. **Did any convergence answer appear that is not in the oracle's set?**
+   **Flag it** — with the caveat in the next section.
+
+## What `f1/n` means here, and why the convergence set is not saturated
+
+`f1/n` is the Good-Turing missing-mass estimate: `f1` is the number of outcomes
+seen exactly once, `n` the sample size, and the ratio estimates the chance the
+next sample produces something never seen before.
+
+The single-run oracle was sampled until `f1/n ≤ 1e-4`. The convergence set was
+**not**, and this is a deliberate choice rather than an oversight:
+
+| | single-run `f1/n` | convergence `f1/n` | unseen answers expected per 100 episodes |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|
+| `run6` | 0.00008 | 0.0160 | 1.6 |
+| `fig5.4-top` | 0.00037 | 0.0140 | 1.4 |
+| `misc3`, `misc2`, `eqe-baaab` | ~0.0002 | 0.0080 | 0.8 |
+| eight problems incl. all of `copy1`–`copy4` | 0.00000 | 0.0000 | 0.0 |
+
+So a 100-episode comparison across all 19 problems will surface **roughly eight
+convergence answers the oracle never sampled, even when nothing has changed.**
+
+That is accepted. Novel convergence answers are a signal the research programme
+wants to see — they are what a changed parameter, a new Slipnet structure or a
+new codelet is expected to produce. Driving `f1/n` to 1e-4 here would cost
+enormously more sampling and would suppress the very thing being looked for. The
+number is published per problem so a reader can weigh a flag against it: a novel
+answer on `copy2` (`f1/n` = 0.0000) is far more interesting than one on `run6`
+(0.0160).
+
+**Read the two flags together.** A missing p50 answer is a strong signal on every
+problem. A novel answer is a strong signal on the eight saturated problems and a
+weak one on `run6` and `fig5.4-top`.
+
+## What carries forward inside an episode, and what does not
+
+Only the contents of Episodic Memory. `init-mcat` (`run.ss:185-238`) reseeds the
+RNG, re-initialises the coderack, temperature, trace and themespace, rebuilds the
+workspace, and sends `reset` to every Slipnet node. Of memory it sends only
+`clear-activations` and `unhighlight-all-answers`, never `clear`.
+
+Memory changes a run at three sites, all keyed on the current problem's own
+strings, so it only bites when the same problem recurs — which is what an episode
+is:
+
+- `answers.ss:982` — the answer-finder fizzles if `answer-present?` says this
+  exact (answer, top rule, bottom rule) is already stored, so a result cannot be
+  reported twice in one episode;
+- `answers.ss:1162` — a snag is recorded only if new;
+- `answers.ss:290,304` — an answer is linked to a remembered snag.
+
+**`answer-present?` includes the rules** (`memory.ss:90-96`). The same answer
+string reached by a *different* rule is not suppressed, so it can and does recur
+within an episode — `fig5.4-top` produces `qeeq` several times in one episode for
+this reason. The episode data records answers only, not rules, so such a
+recurrence is indistinguishable from a plain repeat. This is self-consistent as
+long as the program under test is measured the same way; it would need the rule
+recorded to mean "the engine found something it considered new".
+
+## Seeds
+
+The two data sets use disjoint seed ranges, so no episode run repeats a single
+run already in the oracle:
+
+| | seeds |
+|---|---|
+| single runs | 1 … 51,000 (per problem) |
+| episodes | from 100,000; episode *e* run *i* uses `100000 + e*8 + i` |
+
+Episode index fully determines its seeds, so any episode is reproducible on its
+own, and any single run inside one is reproducible in isolation.
+
+## Regenerating
+
+```sh
+# single-run oracle -- hours
+docker compose exec metacat /metacat/docker/oracle.py
+
+# episodes -- about two hours at 20 concurrent shards
+docker compose exec metacat /metacat/docker/episodes.py
+
+# the four derived sets -- seconds, pure post-processing
+docker/make_derived_sets.py
+```
+
+`episodes.py` fixes its shard count at launch, so a problem whose tail outlives
+the others leaves cores idle. Re-partitioning the remaining episodes across more
+processes is safe and was done for `misc3` — episode index determines the seeds,
+and only episodes with their completion marker are ever read, so an episode
+interrupted mid-flight is redone rather than half-counted. A `--resume` mode
+along those lines is worth adding.
+
+## Provenance
+
+Both data sets were produced after `bf06847`, which repaired
+`constituent-objects-of`. Twelve problems' episode data predates `ca8f7e0`,
+which added five workspace-string methods a snag object needs; those twelve
+recorded zero bad message sends across 48,000 runs, so the repaired path never
+fired for them. Each result in `oracle/raw/episodes.json.gz` carries a `build` field saying
+which side of that fix it came from.
