+# Metacat
+
+James Marshall's Metacat 1.2, with a working toolchain, a headless mode, and a
+measured reference data set.
+
+Metacat is a self-watching analogy-making architecture — Copycat plus an
+Episodic Memory, a Temporal Trace and a themespace, so that it can notice and
+comment on its own behaviour. See
+<http://science.slc.edu/~jmarshall/metacat> and Marshall's `README.txt`.
+
+This repository exists to serve a port, [Petacat](../Petacat). Its job is to say
+what the reference implementation actually *does*, densely enough that another
+program's behaviour can be compared against it.
+
+## Documents
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| [`ORACLE-USAGE.md`](ORACLE-USAGE.md) | What the reference data sets are, and the protocol for comparing another program against them. **Start here** if you are testing a port. |
+| [`CODE-FIXES.md`](CODE-FIXES.md) | Every difference between this tree and Marshall's 1.2, and which of them change measured behaviour. **Start here** if you are wondering whether this is still Metacat. |
+| [`CLAUDE.md`](CLAUDE.md) | Working notes and conventions for this repository. |
+| `README.txt` | Marshall's original, unchanged. |
+
+## Running it
+
+**With the GUI**, as originally distributed — Chez Scheme + SWL + Tcl/Tk, all of
+it containerised:
+
+```sh
+docker compose up --build          # first run, ~10 min
+open http://localhost:6080/vnc.html?autoconnect=1&resize=scale
+```
+
+**Headless**, for measurement — plain Chez, no SWL, no display, ~100× faster:
+
+```sh
+scheme -q --script metacat-headless.ss    # loads the engine and nothing else
+docker/solve.sh abc abd xyz --seed 42     # one problem, one run
+```
+
+Both modes load the same engine. See [`CODE-FIXES.md`](CODE-FIXES.md) §2 for how
+the split works.
+
+## The reference data
+
+```
+oracle/
+  raw/        what the reference emitted -- never edited
+    single-runs.json      374,500 runs, every stopping state
+    episodes.json.gz      9,500 episodes x 8 runs, every run
+  derived/    recomputed from raw/ by docker/make_derived_sets.py
+    single-run-sets.json      the state set per problem
+    single-run-p50.json       its p50 subset, with absence probabilities
+    convergence-sets.json     the episodic final-answer set per problem
+    convergence-p50.json      its p50 subset
+  work/       per-problem shard files -- reproducible, not committed
+```
+
+**Single runs** sample one run from a fresh Episodic Memory, to Good-Turing
+saturation. **Episodes** sample a *session*: eight runs over one problem with
+memory carried forward, so the program remembers what it has already answered.
+The convergence set is the distribution of what those sessions settled on.
+
+A comparison run does 100 tries per problem in each mode and asks two questions
+of each: did every p50 member appear, and did anything appear the reference never
+produced. Both **flag** rather than fail — Metacat is stochastic with no ground
+truth, so the data says *stable or changed*, never *pass or fail*.
+[`ORACLE-USAGE.md`](ORACLE-USAGE.md) has the details, including why the
+convergence set is deliberately left unsaturated.
+
+## Regenerating
+
+```sh
+docker compose exec metacat /metacat/docker/oracle.py      # hours
+docker compose exec metacat /metacat/docker/episodes.py    # ~2 hours
+docker/make_derived_sets.py                                # seconds
+```
+
+## Harness scripts
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| `docker/oracle.py` | single-run benchmark, sharded, Good-Turing stop rule |
+| `docker/episodes.py` | episode benchmark, sharded across episodes |
+| `docker/make_derived_sets.py` | the four derived sets, from `oracle/raw/` |
+| `docker/check_messages.py` | no object is sent a message it cannot answer — run after any engine change |
+| `docker/check_saturation.py` | how complete the reference sets are, and the spurious-flag rate that follows |
+| `docker/episodes_finish.py` | fill an episode run's gaps across more processes; rebuild `episodes.json` |
+| `docker/make_oracle_report.py` | human-readable single-run report |
+| `docker/episode_report.py` | human-readable episode report |
+| `docker/solve.sh` | one problem, one run, no GUI |
+| `docker/batch.py` | ad-hoc batches |
+| `docker/profile_messages.py` | message-frequency profile |
